#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186187910 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "111da4c7-612b-5e1f-a105-2dbaaea4e5a5" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Polar Curves Using the Double-Angle Formula for Sine"}] :question/id #uuid "deaef5cc-99fb-57e5-a6f2-22f17a7f00af" :question/math-academy-id "e-10033" :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r =-2 \\cos\\theta$, shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3854/Source/Images/dc31fd8c1852c7552760d954f4a6b91b.png)" :question/worked-solution "To find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nwe use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\nr=-2\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= -2\\cos θ\\cos θ =-2\\cos^{2} θ, \\\\\ny &= -2\\cos θ\\sin θ =-\\sin 2θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity\n\n$$\n\\sin2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate $x'(\\theta)$ and $y'(\\theta)$.\n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta$:\n$x^{′}(θ)|= (-2\\cos^{2} θ)^{′}; =-2 \\cdot 2\\cos θ \\cdot (-\\sin θ); = 4\\sin θ\\cos θ; = 2\\sin 2θ$\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta$:\n$y^{′}(θ)|= (-\\sin 2θ)^{′}; = - (\\cos 2θ \\cdot 2); =-2\\cos 2θ$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= (y^{′}(θ))/(x^{′}(θ)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-2\\cos 2θ}{2\\sin 2θ} \\\\\n&=-\\cot 2θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188128 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14b618a5-02d2-5564-a288-3423a873a746" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Polar Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "366bcb1d-3fe4-5047-8306-bd28935b5431" :question/math-academy-id "e-631" :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}$ for the polar curve $r=5$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} = (\\frac{d}{dθ}(\\frac{dy}{dx}))/((\\frac{dx}{dθ}))\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta\n$$\n\nas parametric equations of a curve in terms of $\\theta$.\n\nUsing the fact that $r=5$, we get\n\n$$\nx =5\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=5\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nThen, to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nwe use the rule\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{′}(θ) &= -5\\sin θ, \\\\\ny^{′}(θ) &= 5\\cos θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{5\\cos θ}{-5\\sin θ} \\\\\n&=-\\cot θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= (\\frac{d}{dθ}(-\\cot θ))/(-5\\sin θ) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{5}\\frac{\\csc^{2} θ}{\\sin θ} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{5}\\csc^{2} θ \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sin θ} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{5}\\csc^{2} θ \\cdot \\csc θ \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{5}\\csc^{3} θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188903 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "111da4c7-612b-5e1f-a105-2dbaaea4e5a5" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Polar Curves Using the Double-Angle Formula for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd928b77-2c02-501f-b255-ecab853a114f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cot 2θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187899} {:answer/id #uuid "d3bec273-088e-5276-8ed7-1d14cc7e27f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan 2θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187900} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c50b47-5ff2-536a-9173-bf87819ae207" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\cot 2θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187901} {:answer/id #uuid "4eb08f01-94a9-5212-8871-2c917027ca81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\tan 2θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187902} {:answer/id #uuid "699f899d-e6c7-5b85-ab74-0bb458e45a25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\tan θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8c50b47-5ff2-536a-9173-bf87819ae207" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\cot 2θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187901} :answer-field/id #uuid "c088d0f4-0714-5f57-bbfb-380defa14744" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187898}] :question/id #uuid "a7256ac7-6551-5109-b9b8-6e1497bbcfbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-9388" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3854/Source/Images/q-9388.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ for the polar curve $r = \\cos θ$, shown above."}]}
